I'm not just talking about single dungeon levels existing at multiple
elevations, and I'm not just talking about aerial combat.  IMVHO, I think
that not much can be done vertically with roguelikes because they exist
mainly, if not completely, in two dimensions.

There are several major reasons that NetHack and other roguelikes are the
games that many people have grown to enjoy.  Firstly, they are built with
specific user interface limitations.  The best example is the 2D map based
on a 2D grid layout.  The second reason is because the DevTeam has fleshed
out many (if not all) of the possible details for the game given the
specific limitations or "parameters" that the game has to work with.

The idea I'm suggesting would be to make a game that is truly 3D, and let
the development flesh out the details given by the parameter of using cubes
instead of squares/rectangles as a unit of space.  Then gradual development
and playtesting could determine more of what can be done with vertical
distance.

I suppose a finished product would be easier comment on then an initial
design idea... but this is what could be considered "market research".


